      "content": "Ah, cleaning up legacy data is like trying to herd a bunch of mischievous goblins into a tidy little line. First, you'll want to cast a wide net with your queries to catch all the goblins hiding in the corners. Look for repeating patterns, like the same goblin appearing in multiple tables, or the same goblin wearing different hats. You might find that some tables are just echo chambers, repeating the same goblin tales over and over again.\n\nNext, you'll want to map out the relationships between your tables, like drawing a map of the goblin kingdom. This will help you spot where goblins are being counted twice or where they're supposed to be in different places. Once you've got that map, you can start to reorganize the goblins into their proper homes, ensuring each table has a unique role and nothing is being counted twice.\n\nRemember, when you're dealing with goblins, it's always better to have a few well-organized goblins than a bunch of goblins running wild. So, take your time, and don't be afraid to ask for help from your fellow goblin handlers. After all, even the most experienced goblin wranglers need a little help now and then. Good luck, and may your",
